1
00:00:03,070 --> 00:00:07,961
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a few more questions that are kind of suitable for reflection.

2
00:00:11,914 --> 00:00:34,137
[SPEAKER_00]: I came up and I thought maybe we can just, like, address and go through, like, practical, like, how to practically think about and reflect on certain topics that can really be the the basis for the right type of meditation, instead of, you know, the the common self-hypnotic

3
00:00:35,598 --> 00:00:37,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Attitudes and outcomes.

4
00:00:38,919 --> 00:00:39,740
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like...

5
00:00:40,540 --> 00:00:51,586
[SPEAKER_00]: I think one of the most fundamental things, really, that everybody should be clear about with themselves, it's... Why do I meditate?

6
00:00:51,626 --> 00:00:53,367
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, what is the purpose?

7
00:00:54,028 --> 00:00:57,810
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the outcome I'm hoping for to...

8
00:00:59,781 --> 00:01:01,822
[SPEAKER_00]: As a result of my meditation.

9
00:01:02,082 --> 00:01:05,363
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever that meditation is, doesn't matter.

10
00:01:05,403 --> 00:01:06,864
[SPEAKER_00]: We don't have to get into that now here.

11
00:01:07,784 --> 00:01:08,644
[SPEAKER_00]: The rights and wrongs.

12
00:01:08,664 --> 00:01:13,446
[SPEAKER_00]: We have plenty of talks that deal with those things.

13
00:01:13,526 --> 00:01:23,670
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's more like just whatever notion of meditation that you have, like what is the reason behind it.

14
00:01:25,259 --> 00:01:27,661
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is that something you're clear about?

15
00:01:28,182 --> 00:01:37,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that something that you clearly see, kind of, the path outlined, required, the outcomes of it?

16
00:01:38,231 --> 00:01:41,014
[SPEAKER_00]: And where are those motivations rooted in?

17
00:01:41,194 --> 00:01:42,635
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what do you hope to achieve?

18
00:01:43,956 --> 00:01:45,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I think very often

19
00:01:47,043 --> 00:01:59,174
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, people read the suttas, get the sense of contemporary Buddhism, various teachers, instructions, find meditation techniques and so on, and it's helpful.

20
00:01:59,694 --> 00:02:06,341
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it does kind of always go hand in hand with these Buddhist instructions that are found in the suttas.

21
00:02:06,361 --> 00:02:08,543
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, they talk about freedom and so on.

22
00:02:10,204 --> 00:02:32,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Freedom from suffering, freedom from desire, freedom from hindrances, but I think more often than not, if you think about it today, like on today's terms, just that notion of freedom, it's quite ambiguous, because people haven't really, you know, sat and philosophically really get to the bottom of it.

23
00:02:32,718 --> 00:02:34,619
[SPEAKER_00]: What does it mean to be free, for example?

24
00:02:36,165 --> 00:02:37,246
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just freedom.

25
00:02:37,547 --> 00:02:39,610
[SPEAKER_00]: It's, you know, feels like this.

26
00:02:39,710 --> 00:02:48,441
[SPEAKER_00]: I have some notions, but more often than not, it's simply misrepresented, misconceived and confused.

27
00:02:49,398 --> 00:02:50,078
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh...

28
00:02:50,239 --> 00:03:05,889
[SPEAKER_00]: I think, practically speaking, if you were to take a sample of your, like, average, average meditators and so on, I think more people would be confusing just a notion of, eh... peace, whatever, whatever has, eh...

29
00:03:06,549 --> 00:03:11,150
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever has kind of enabled me to get some sense of peace, they would confuse that for freedom.

30
00:03:11,290 --> 00:03:14,691
[SPEAKER_00]: They would think, oh, that's the freedom that the Buddha was talking about in the suttas.

31
00:03:14,771 --> 00:03:23,853
[SPEAKER_00]: So... so that's... therefore, when I sit and meditate free from distractions, free from things that disturb me in a... in a controlled environment, I am peaceful.

32
00:03:25,133 --> 00:03:29,514
[SPEAKER_00]: And that... that equals freedom, or that's bringing me closer to freedom.

33
00:03:30,364 --> 00:03:48,909
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, as I said before so many times, if you actually look at the suttas correctly, as a whole body of instruction, as the broadest context of instruction that the Buddha gave to people, yeah, the peace is kind of the result of freedom, not other way around.

34
00:03:50,429 --> 00:03:57,331
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might have peaceful experiences here and there, but if you're confusing peace with freedom, means you would be

35
00:03:58,695 --> 00:04:11,420
[SPEAKER_00]: More often than not, confusing a mere agreeable circumstances, comfort, with things up to your liking or within your control, you will confuse that for a path of freedom.

36
00:04:12,080 --> 00:04:13,861
[SPEAKER_00]: And that that couldn't be further from the truth.

37
00:04:16,968 --> 00:04:43,175
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's kind of what happens, basically, when such confusion exists, when such misrepresentation exists in people, they would be merely pretty much going around hunting refined pleasures, refined peacefulness, and at the pretext of freedom, freedom from suffering, and so on, and other things that let's talk about, but in reality, just kind of

38
00:04:44,175 --> 00:04:47,359
[SPEAKER_00]: Creating this this mask of an underlying agitation.

39
00:04:49,521 --> 00:05:04,017
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by equating the peaceful state, we could put it that way, I guess, by equating the peaceful state, you kind of end up still acting out of the the same cravings that were

40
00:05:05,647 --> 00:05:08,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Disturbing your peace, like in any other scenario.

41
00:05:08,908 --> 00:05:20,030
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, your pursuit of the peaceful states become like a subtle indulgence in a... very much in like a... in a central domain, in the form of central craving.

42
00:05:21,150 --> 00:05:31,092
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like a coarsely going after the desires, but the same motivation that you have when you desire sense objects or when you desire to act out of ill-will.

43
00:05:31,752 --> 00:05:34,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, the same desire to be peaceful

44
00:05:36,845 --> 00:05:38,526
[SPEAKER_00]: That's confused for freedom or whatever.

45
00:05:39,106 --> 00:05:41,968
[SPEAKER_00]: That's basically... that desire is still the central desire.

46
00:05:43,249 --> 00:06:01,000
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if you invest like years and years of effort in like building this this elaborate environment and system that supports the practice of peace, that supports the non-disturbance, that supports pretty much all the circumstances that might be involved in it,

47
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:18,291
[SPEAKER_00]: If that original motivation and that desire is never questioned towards peaceful states as valuable, it's never questioned, never reflected upon, it's... the whole thing will still be a projection within the central domain.

48
00:06:18,371 --> 00:06:20,313
[SPEAKER_00]: It will be still rooted in central craving.

49
00:06:22,141 --> 00:06:41,352
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why, more often than not, people today would train rightly if they were enduring the friction of the present moment, of the things that are kind of pressuring you, disturbing you, then just going blindly or at face value following peaceful states.

50
00:06:43,533 --> 00:06:48,496
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if you do get peaceful states, you put them in a bracket, so to speak.

51
00:06:48,556 --> 00:06:51,558
[SPEAKER_00]: You step back and you don't lose perspective in regard to that.

52
00:06:52,766 --> 00:07:04,234
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's like all of this, as I often keep saying, can be realigned correctly and rekindled through right questioning.

53
00:07:05,555 --> 00:07:15,942
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you find meditation helpful, you find sitting peacefully helpful, that's fine, but you gotta not take that for granted and also try and understand it.

54
00:07:16,343 --> 00:07:16,543
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

55
00:07:17,721 --> 00:07:18,381
[SPEAKER_00]: But not...

56
00:07:18,401 --> 00:07:23,082
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't even understand peace, why peace is peaceful, but I understand why is it that what you want.

57
00:07:24,522 --> 00:07:30,824
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why do you think the practice is about being peaceful, for example?

58
00:07:32,024 --> 00:07:32,904
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, it's not like a...

59
00:07:33,444 --> 00:07:35,425
[SPEAKER_00]: I need to find the right answer, the wrong answer.

60
00:07:35,845 --> 00:07:39,925
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like I need to find the answer that pertains to my motivation.

61
00:07:39,945 --> 00:07:41,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, why do I want this?

62
00:07:42,106 --> 00:07:44,086
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what do I think freedom is?

63
00:07:44,186 --> 00:07:45,687
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I think meditation is?

64
00:07:45,967 --> 00:07:47,187
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I think practice is?

65
00:07:48,220 --> 00:07:57,592
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when I dutifully keep the precepts and sit and meditate or go on retreat and stuff, what exactly am I hoping to achieve?

66
00:07:57,673 --> 00:08:06,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I see that path of achievement clearly outlined, as in like, oh, if I do this, I clearly understand the results of it?

67
00:08:07,798 --> 00:08:13,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it all based on kind of a vague notion of, well, something good will happen out of this.

68
00:08:13,682 --> 00:08:15,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Something good will come out of this.

69
00:08:15,823 --> 00:08:20,486
[SPEAKER_00]: And in the meantime, I prefer it because I feel peaceful and comfortable.

70
00:08:20,526 --> 00:08:22,587
[SPEAKER_00]: I think more often than not, that's another thing.

71
00:08:23,228 --> 00:08:34,315
[SPEAKER_00]: See, if people confuse the peace for freedom, as in any peace they can get by any means necessary for freedom, they will inevitably

72
00:08:35,303 --> 00:08:44,474
[SPEAKER_00]: I fall into a trap of pretty much needing and preferring comfort and confusing that for the practice.

73
00:08:44,614 --> 00:08:49,560
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when I live in the environment, when there are no disturbances,

74
00:08:50,722 --> 00:08:57,824
[SPEAKER_00]: When there are no physical endeavors, because usually like most of the... even like most of the monasteries and stuff become that.

75
00:08:58,504 --> 00:09:15,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Many of the places become like very kind of precious and padded around the environment, so people become more and more and more and more sheltered, and that practically results just living in a constant... in a comfort that you couldn't have pretty much afforded even as a layperson.

76
00:09:16,589 --> 00:09:19,710
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, like comfort... when I say comfort, I don't mean like...

77
00:09:21,003 --> 00:09:36,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Luxurious beds and stuff like that, sure, that can easily be done with, but it's more like... it's quite a privilege to be able to live in the environment where there are no sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches bombarding you.

78
00:09:38,254 --> 00:09:48,681
[SPEAKER_00]: So... but do you take that within the right context or do you take that for granted and just start to depend now on the environment when there are no provocations?

79
00:09:50,355 --> 00:09:54,818
[SPEAKER_00]: The environment that's comfortable, because you don't have to deal with anything, because nothing comes up, because there's nothing disturbing you.

80
00:09:57,399 --> 00:10:05,504
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not a problem to seek a suitable, peaceful environment, but if you're doing it for the wrong reasons, it will lead to nowhere anyway.

81
00:10:06,485 --> 00:10:15,150
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point is, like, like, are you afraid, for example, are you afraid of being disturbed by the external circumstances?

82
00:10:15,430 --> 00:10:18,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, would that undermine your meditation?

83
00:10:19,434 --> 00:10:21,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Would that threaten it?

84
00:10:22,876 --> 00:10:25,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, that's not the meditation you should be cultivating.

85
00:10:26,578 --> 00:10:40,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, the whole samādhi, the principle behind, the suttas often talk about, it's like immovability, imperturbability, in face of sights that want to move you, smells that want to move you, touches and so on.

86
00:10:41,306 --> 00:10:46,209
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, physical withdrawal necessary in order to support the precepts,

87
00:10:47,157 --> 00:10:56,848
[SPEAKER_00]: But, the work still needs to be done towards the actual freedom of imperturbability, and not fall into a trap of, oh, I've withdrawn myself from sensuality.

88
00:10:58,132 --> 00:11:16,917
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, I live in a controlled environment where there are, most of the time, there are no distractions, no bombardments of sensuality, no disturbances, no physical work, no labor, not really having to endure sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, heat, cold, all those things that suttas talk about.

89
00:11:17,437 --> 00:11:22,678
[SPEAKER_00]: And then people would kind of fall into that sort of trap of

90
00:11:25,354 --> 00:11:43,790
[SPEAKER_00]: being completely dependent on that very artificial environment and taking for granted and even confusing it for peace, because there's nothing disturbing me here, therefore I'm not disturbed, therefore for me there is no work to do, even if obviously internally people know that there is plenty of work to do.

91
00:11:44,891 --> 00:11:45,612
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why it's like

92
00:11:47,508 --> 00:12:01,614
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to start questioning your motivation way before you become that dependent on these artificial circumstances, that... because, again, you're depending on comfort, you're depending on sense of control, and that's not going to be easy to undo.

93
00:12:03,595 --> 00:12:16,080
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you sit and meditate, or you sit and contemplate, another thing you could be asking yourself, am I truly, actually, seeking liberation that the Buddha was talking about?

94
00:12:18,139 --> 00:12:20,771
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, is that what I am aiming at?

95
00:12:25,184 --> 00:12:31,608
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, am I just, deep down, really just want a slightly better way to hide from suffering when it comes?

96
00:12:32,528 --> 00:12:35,870
[SPEAKER_00]: A slightly stronger lid to keep on top of it?

97
00:12:36,210 --> 00:12:37,631
[SPEAKER_00]: A slightly better way to manage?

98
00:12:38,652 --> 00:12:40,933
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, what do I want, basically?

99
00:12:41,153 --> 00:12:43,494
[SPEAKER_00]: That's pretty much what you should be asking yourself.

100
00:12:43,554 --> 00:12:52,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, when I'm doing this practice, when I'm practicing the dhamma, when I'm studying the suttas, when I'm meditating, when I'm contemplating, what do I want from it?

101
00:12:52,999 --> 00:12:53,840
[SPEAKER_00]: What is my goal?

102
00:12:55,028 --> 00:12:56,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, how far do I want to go?

103
00:12:57,630 --> 00:12:59,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I even... have I thought about it?

104
00:13:02,873 --> 00:13:12,439
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as I said, if these motivations are never revealed, they will be aligned with, pretty much, the place where you come from.

105
00:13:12,459 --> 00:13:16,321
[SPEAKER_00]: And the place you come from, it's always going to be in the beginning the place of desire, the place of sensuality.

106
00:13:17,062 --> 00:13:23,286
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the more you do your practices of the dhamma, whatever you think those are, without

107
00:13:25,223 --> 00:13:42,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Unraveling and revealing these, as I said, the context of your motivation as to why you're doing in the first place, what's your tacit kind of assumptions and implications, then everything you do in the in the name of practice, your meditation, the precepts, everything, it's just going to become basically a

108
00:13:44,792 --> 00:14:00,382
[SPEAKER_00]: An extension, like a subtle continuation of the subtle addiction to the sense of control, to comfort, to the preferences, to avoiding pain, prolonging pleasure.

109
00:14:01,123 --> 00:14:05,625
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it just becomes very much the extension of the same thing.

110
00:14:05,926 --> 00:14:07,347
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't really uproot anything.

111
00:14:09,248 --> 00:14:13,370
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, again, through the questioning, you would reveal this motivation.

112
00:14:13,470 --> 00:14:13,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, when

113
00:14:16,388 --> 00:14:20,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Your intentions behind why you're meditating, why you're doing this, become somewhat clearer.

114
00:14:20,411 --> 00:14:25,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, new questions will arise, or new questions will present themselves.

115
00:14:25,434 --> 00:14:44,308
[SPEAKER_00]: So, am I seeking peace with a view that such peace and freedom will come on account of full understanding, say, of suffering, of feelings, of craving, fully training my mind out of the desire?

116
00:14:45,602 --> 00:15:04,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Or do I deep down still carry an assumption that that peace will just sort of be bestowed upon me, if I do these random things rightly, long enough, I will have a breakthrough, which is kind of a form of like an instant gratification of what you've been working for, like a reward.

117
00:15:05,353 --> 00:15:11,937
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm doing this, I'm doing this, I don't see the connection, I don't see the clear... like there's no clear understanding, as I said, of the outcomes and so on.

118
00:15:12,697 --> 00:15:20,646
[SPEAKER_00]: But I'm just doing it because it makes some sense, I learned about it, and I'm waiting for the peace, for liberation, for whatever you want to call it, to happen to me.

119
00:15:21,788 --> 00:15:32,400
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the difference between, like, are you actually developing it as something that you understood how to be developed, or you're just doing random stuff and then hoping that development was just going to come your way.

120
00:15:34,868 --> 00:15:48,855
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's like... and, yeah, say, if you ask these questions as a part of your meditation and contemplation, you might get different answers at different times.

121
00:15:50,395 --> 00:15:56,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, sometimes your motivation might be leaning one way, sometimes might be the other way, sometimes old views might creep in.

122
00:15:56,598 --> 00:15:59,620
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, again, maintaining the clarity of that context

123
00:16:01,193 --> 00:16:08,157
[SPEAKER_00]: And broadening it, in that sense, with a further clarification, is something that you can't have too much of.

124
00:16:08,257 --> 00:16:11,019
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Sutta said, like you can't have too much of sati, basically.

125
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:21,944
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, see, all of these other subtle and tacit little views that you might have here and there, are going to start floating up to the surface.

126
00:16:23,365 --> 00:16:24,326
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like another sea.

127
00:16:24,806 --> 00:16:26,767
[SPEAKER_00]: You start questioning yourself, why am I meditating?

128
00:16:29,178 --> 00:16:34,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I really, you know, seeking freedom from suffering the way the suttas outline it?

129
00:16:36,562 --> 00:16:50,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, you know, I just want to feel basically peaceful and undisturbed here and now, but I'm not really willing to give up too many things.

130
00:16:52,549 --> 00:16:57,512
[SPEAKER_00]: That already shows you, like, what direction your mind is leaning, what direction the animal is preferring.

131
00:16:58,868 --> 00:17:04,274
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you can already contrast that with the instruction that the Buddha gave and say, well, is that aligned with what the Buddha said?

132
00:17:04,454 --> 00:17:09,439
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that aligned with, you know, countless of statements of saying sensuality is an obstruction?

133
00:17:11,381 --> 00:17:14,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensual desires are peril, they're in the way.

134
00:17:15,226 --> 00:17:18,389
[SPEAKER_00]: Acting out of will-will is unwholesome, it's ignoble and stuff.

135
00:17:20,036 --> 00:17:28,838
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you could already then see whether you are trying to practice dhamma on your own terms or you are actually trying to align it with how it's presented in the suttas.

136
00:17:28,898 --> 00:17:39,560
[SPEAKER_00]: Because then that kind of leads to natural questions like, okay, so, how about if I practice the dhamma or say, how about I'm seeking?

137
00:17:39,800 --> 00:17:43,000
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's fine to want to be peaceful and it's fine to want to be happy.

138
00:17:43,020 --> 00:17:43,841
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no problem with that.

139
00:17:44,841 --> 00:17:46,041
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just how you go about it.

140
00:17:47,640 --> 00:17:57,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you just acting to... are you just sort of investing your efforts that are still very much driven and rooted by the same central desire that you are completely refusing to question?

141
00:17:59,809 --> 00:18:00,870
[SPEAKER_00]: Emotional preferences?

142
00:18:01,511 --> 00:18:03,332
[SPEAKER_00]: Or are you willing to go against those if needed?

143
00:18:04,713 --> 00:18:08,016
[SPEAKER_00]: So then the question you should be asking... you could be asking yourself if you want.

144
00:18:08,076 --> 00:18:09,637
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you don't have to do any of this.

145
00:18:10,337 --> 00:18:13,360
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you want, it's like, could I

146
00:18:14,795 --> 00:18:26,044
[SPEAKER_00]: Even imagine, say, being happy, being content, being peaceful even, yeah, without needing to do anything on account of anything that happens to me.

147
00:18:28,846 --> 00:18:33,610
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is that a possibility for me?

148
00:18:34,010 --> 00:18:35,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, is that a possibility for my mind?

149
00:18:36,632 --> 00:18:38,554
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's not, can I imagine it?

150
00:18:38,774 --> 00:18:40,195
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I imagine the benefits of it?

151
00:18:41,036 --> 00:18:42,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Would that be a beneficial skill to develop?

152
00:18:44,132 --> 00:19:12,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, genuinely, it doesn't matter what comes your way, doesn't matter what happens to you, doesn't matter who says what, what you experience, whether you lose or you gain or whatever worldly wind blows blows your way, could you remain peaceful without needing to deal with anything that comes your way, without needing to move, without needing to do anything, without needing to act out, without needing to manage, without needing to counter,

153
00:19:13,930 --> 00:19:18,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Could you remain untouched, pretty much?

154
00:19:19,274 --> 00:19:20,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you imagine how that would feel?

155
00:19:21,195 --> 00:19:22,456
[SPEAKER_00]: Would that be a valuable thing?

156
00:19:22,496 --> 00:19:24,557
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, would it be a valuable thing to develop?

157
00:19:32,162 --> 00:19:37,185
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you can ask yourself, okay, so... How would one go about developing that?

158
00:19:38,246 --> 00:19:38,847
[SPEAKER_00]: How would I do it?

159
00:19:39,527 --> 00:19:40,848
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if somebody were to ask you,

160
00:19:43,005 --> 00:19:44,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Hey, how would you develop that?

161
00:19:44,726 --> 00:19:46,667
[SPEAKER_00]: What comes to your mind as an answer?

162
00:19:48,528 --> 00:19:49,849
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, how is it that you understand?

163
00:19:50,789 --> 00:20:02,036
[SPEAKER_00]: The imperturbability, immovability, contentment, peace, being above the suffering, like the lotus is above the water and the mud that it grew out of, and stuff like that.

164
00:20:02,076 --> 00:20:03,757
[SPEAKER_00]: All the stuff they talk about.

165
00:20:04,575 --> 00:20:12,179
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, can you wrap your head around it without, like, as I said, just resorting to, like, an answer, a line from the suttas or something?

166
00:20:12,780 --> 00:20:15,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, no, you're doing this as a contemplation, as a reflection.

167
00:20:15,201 --> 00:20:16,142
[SPEAKER_00]: When you sit quietly,

168
00:20:17,274 --> 00:20:38,823
[SPEAKER_00]: As a part of your meditation, you actually want to be uncovering and revealing these very, sort of, views that you carry, inclinations, leanings, preferences, because that is how sati is developed, that's how sati is broadened, that's how sati becomes... the context becomes more established.

169
00:20:39,223 --> 00:20:41,584
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, so, like, can I be

170
00:20:44,598 --> 00:20:45,099
[SPEAKER_00]: Untouched.

171
00:20:45,599 --> 00:20:54,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I be... or, say, even if I can't be untouched, I can't imagine how it would feel to be untouched by unpleasant things and so on, can I remain immovable?

172
00:20:55,328 --> 00:21:00,773
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, see, you might not have a choice whether you're touched or not, but you certainly have a choice whether you're going to act out of it or not.

173
00:21:02,374 --> 00:21:10,462
[SPEAKER_00]: So, do you see, do you recognize, can you discern the threshold, the extent of

174
00:21:11,906 --> 00:21:21,271
[SPEAKER_00]: How far you could go, how far you could take on without needing to, you know, act out of panic or pressure or hindrance.

175
00:21:22,092 --> 00:21:25,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, where is that line?

176
00:21:25,453 --> 00:21:39,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it as soon as something unpleasant touches you, your mind spirals out of control and you're just acting out of all of these habits, firing on all cylinders just to cover it up, just to manage, just to regain the sense of pleasure and control and comfort and get rid of the pain?

177
00:21:40,041 --> 00:21:41,322
[SPEAKER_00]: Or are you able to stay with it?

178
00:21:42,733 --> 00:21:44,774
[SPEAKER_00]: So, are you able to stay with the discomfort?

179
00:21:45,454 --> 00:21:52,656
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you able to stay with, like, mental threats that your own mind can be showing you, throwing at you?

180
00:21:56,577 --> 00:21:57,218
[SPEAKER_00]: And where is the light?

181
00:21:57,258 --> 00:21:58,558
[SPEAKER_00]: How far you can stay with it?

182
00:22:00,099 --> 00:22:10,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, it's like these things need to be, like, you need to shine the light onto them through these reflections and questions, because otherwise they will remain, like, hidden

183
00:22:11,507 --> 00:22:25,146
[SPEAKER_00]: As a guiding principle behind your efforts, investments and everything else, because you're not looking at them, they're there in the background influencing through preferences, through leanings, through these subtle cravings.

184
00:22:26,348 --> 00:22:27,009
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the point.

185
00:22:27,049 --> 00:22:27,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you don't

186
00:22:29,017 --> 00:22:34,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Uncover the subtle cravings through, like, psychologizing about mechanics of cravings and results and stuff.

187
00:22:34,998 --> 00:22:44,681
[SPEAKER_00]: You uncover them by questioning your own motivation, because craving is always where your desire is, where your intention is, to do this, to do that.

188
00:22:46,001 --> 00:22:58,124
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by questioning that, unravelling, clearing out anything that's sort of covering it up, it's, you know, it can eventually reach a point where things are

189
00:23:00,055 --> 00:23:12,173
[SPEAKER_00]: Clearly understood, on the level that they are, so that when you are touched by pleasure, touched by pain, there is enough of width, so to speak, in your mind,

190
00:23:13,713 --> 00:23:20,719
[SPEAKER_00]: That you wouldn't fall into the trap of immediate reaction, immediate movement, immediate activation.

191
00:23:21,280 --> 00:23:27,065
[SPEAKER_00]: So, see, like, say something touches you, unpleasantly, and then you immediately have the habitual thoughts.

192
00:23:27,525 --> 00:23:31,929
[SPEAKER_00]: Say somebody said something upsetting, and you immediately have the habitual thoughts.

193
00:23:32,549 --> 00:23:36,493
[SPEAKER_00]: There will be just... and then, obviously, you'll be speaking, saying stuff, doing stuff, acting out of it.

194
00:23:36,553 --> 00:23:38,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Say that's somebody who's not restrained.

195
00:23:39,495 --> 00:24:04,527
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you have been practicing, training rightly, restraining, reflecting, as I said, and delving deeper and deeper, or broader and broader, into these contexts and preconceived notions, you will find yourself in a position of something says... somebody says something upsetting to you, the upset is felt, and even the thoughts might start rushing out, but you remain still.

196
00:24:06,007 --> 00:24:06,728
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, oh,

197
00:24:07,894 --> 00:24:12,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Look, all these floods came up, but I was not taken by them.

198
00:24:13,538 --> 00:24:16,419
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, obviously, it's going to give you more confidence.

199
00:24:16,880 --> 00:24:20,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, so can I develop this principle even further, whereby there'll be no even floods coming?

200
00:24:21,262 --> 00:24:27,006
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason the floods were coming is because I was still liable to be kind of taken by them.

201
00:24:27,506 --> 00:24:33,970
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, when I've sufficiently remained and practiced immovability on the level of body, speech, and mind,

202
00:24:36,687 --> 00:24:43,951
[SPEAKER_00]: The floods dry up, basically, because I'm not there anymore, basically, to be taken by those same torrents and so on.

203
00:24:45,852 --> 00:24:55,997
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, that can be a meditation, certainly for some period of time, because, as I said, plenty more questions

204
00:24:58,016 --> 00:25:13,344
[SPEAKER_00]: It will come up if you start asking these things rightly and not immediately jumping in for an answer, as I said, but asking them and then staying in that mode of a question asked without defaulting to an answer that's going to satisfy and plug the hole, so to speak.

205
00:25:13,404 --> 00:25:13,765
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

206
00:25:14,105 --> 00:25:15,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Ask a question and leave it there for a while.

207
00:25:16,026 --> 00:25:16,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't ignore it.

208
00:25:16,986 --> 00:25:21,709
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't start thinking about something else and see what the question points at.

209
00:25:23,609 --> 00:25:33,252
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you start getting the answers that are more and more aligned with how things actually are, with the principles of the five aggregates and everything else that the Buddha talked about.

